  mssh -j ops@10.0.0.1 ./app.tar root@10.0.0.9:/tmp/       # upload
  mssh -j ops@10.0.0.1 root@10.0.0.9:/var/log/syslog ./    # download
  mssh ./app.tar 10.0.0.9:/tmp/           # user defaults here too
  mssh ./a ./b root@10.0.0.9:/opt/pkg/    # several, DEST must be a dir
  mssh -r ./dist root@10.0.0.9:/srv/www/  # -r recurses into directories
  mssh -rp root@10.0.0.9:/etc/nginx ./    # -p also keeps the mtime
  mssh ./x root@10.0.0.9:                 # bare ':' is the remote $HOME
  mssh ./x root@10.0.0.9:36001:/tmp/      # with a port
  The path after ':' may be absolute or relative to the remote home.
  An argument is remote when what precedes its first ':' could be a host
  name, as under scp, so a local file named 'notes:2024.txt' has to be
  written './notes:2024.txt'.  Two spellings are refused rather than
  guessed: 'h:8900' is host and port with no path (write 'h:8900:8900',
  or './h:8900' for a local file), and 'h::8900' leaves the port field
  empty (write 'h:./:8900' for that path, or fill the port in).
  Runs over sftp on the target; add -C to compress large transfers.
  A copied file keeps its permissions, as under scp: an executable stays
  executable, filtered by your umask.  A file that already exists keeps its
  own mode, so overwriting never widens one.  -p asks for the exact mode
  instead, umask and all, plus the modification time.

persistent session (keep one connection and one shell alive):
  mssh --session s -j ops@10.0.0.1 root@10.0.0.9   # first call: detaches
  mssh --session s 'cd /var/log && ls'             # runs in that shell
  mssh --session s 'grep -c error messages'        # exit code is grep's
  mssh --session s 'X=1'; mssh --session s 'echo $X'   # state persists
  Each call is a separate process, but the working directory, variables
  and background jobs live on in the session, which a plain -c cannot do.
  Once a session is live every positional is a command, so a repeated
  start runs the endpoint as one; --status says whether one is live.

  --session may then be left out, because the name of a session is also the
  name of the machine it reached -- it is the whole path, under an alias:
  mssh s 'cd /var/log && ls'          # = mssh --session s 'cd ...'
  mssh s --status                     # and --stop, --interrupt
  mssh s --jobs                       # active commands and transfer channels
  mssh s --exec -t 'vim /etc/hosts'
  mssh s                              # nothing to run: a login
  A word no live session answers to is read as a host, exactly as before,
  so 'mssh 10.0.0.9' still logs in to 10.0.0.9.

  Copy files through an existing session without reconnecting:
  mssh s --put ./app.conf /tmp/app.conf           # LOCAL -> REMOTE
  mssh s --get /var/log/app.log ./app.log         # REMOTE -> LOCAL
  mssh s -rp --put ./release /tmp/                # directory, keep attributes
  --put/--get require SFTP on the target. Add -r for directories and -p
  to preserve permissions and timestamps. Transfers use a separate channel;
  relative remote paths do not follow the session shell's current directory.

  Keep local TCP or Unix socket forwards in the session daemon:
  mssh --session s -L 8080:127.0.0.1:80 root@10.0.0.9
  mssh s -L 15432:db.internal:5432                # configure an existing session
  mssh s -L '[::1]:8080:[::1]:80'                 # IPv6
  mssh s -L 8080:/run/service.sock               # TCP -> remote Unix socket
  mssh s -L /tmp/db.sock:db.internal:5432        # local Unix socket -> TCP
  mssh s -L /tmp/client.sock:/run/service.sock   # Unix socket -> Unix socket
  -L accepts [bind_address:]port:host:hostport,
             [bind_address:]port:remote_socket,
             local_socket:host:hostport, or local_socket:remote_socket.
  Repeat -L for several; a failed bind rolls back that whole addition.
  The client exits after configuration; --jobs lists each forward and
  --interrupt --job ID closes its listener and connections. The destination
  is reached from the target; SSH must permit TCP/streamlocal forwarding.
  Default bind is localhost; * or an empty bind exposes all interfaces.
  Socket paths contain /; use ./name.sock for a relative path. Local paths
  start at the configuring client's directory; remote paths are literal.
  Local sockets are mode 0600, never overwrite paths and are removed on stop.
  --reconnect retains listeners, but existing connections cannot be restored.

  Keep a dynamic SOCKS proxy in the session daemon:
  mssh --session s -D 1080 root@10.0.0.9
  mssh s -D 127.0.0.1:1081                      # add after session startup
  mssh s -D '[::1]:1082'                        # IPv6 listener
  curl --socks5-hostname 127.0.0.1:1080 http://service.internal/
  -D [bind_address:]port supports SOCKS4/4a and SOCKS5 TCP CONNECT,
  with names resolved on the target. Authentication, BIND and UDP are
  not supported. Default bind is localhost; exposing an unauthenticated
  proxy with * or an empty bind lets other machines use your SSH access.
  Repeat -D or combine with -L; all new listeners bind or all roll back.
  Each proxy is a forward job in --jobs; --stop --job ID removes it.

  'mssh s' with nothing to run is 'ssh s' as nearly as this can be: a shell on the
  target, on a channel of its own rather than the session's own shell,
  which is busy being a session -- the framing around every command above
  is what makes it one.  Your terminal drives the shell the way --exec -t
  drives a program (forwarded as its input, raw until it ends, resized
  with your window, no --wait), and the exit code is the shell's.

  stdin is forwarded when it is a pipe or a file, as it would be to ssh:
  echo hello | mssh --session s cat                # prints hello
  mssh --session s 'wc -l' < access.log            # counts the local file
  tar cf - ./dir | mssh --session s 'tar xf - -C /srv'
  It streams, so a filter fed by a slow producer prints as it goes, and the
  command still runs in the session's shell -- a 'cd' before it still
  applies.  A terminal is NOT forwarded by default, since with --stdin the
  command reads until you type Ctrl-D: that is what you want for 'cat', and
  not what you want for a plain 'mssh --session s ls'.  -n never forwards,
  for when mssh's stdin belongs to something else (a script read from a pipe).
  /dev/null is never forwarded: it cannot be told from having no input.

  -c holds an interactive program instead of a shell, and --prompt names
  the regex that ends one of its replies:
  mssh --session g --prompt '\\(gdb\\) $' -j ops@10.0.0.1 root@10.0.0.9 \\
       -c 'gdb -q -ex "set pagination off" -p 1234'
  mssh --session g 'bt'                            # output, then exit 0

  mssh --session s --status    # target, uptime, commands served, link
  --status also shows task counts, default job state and recent activity.
  Use --jobs for commands, channels and -L/-D listener details.
  mssh --session s --interrupt # Ctrl-C, to unstick a hung command
  mssh s --jobs               # list active tasks and their IDs
  mssh s --interrupt --job 3  # interrupt only this task
  mssh s --job 3 --stop       # same targeted cancellation, keep session alive
  mssh --session s --stop      # end it; --sessions lists live ones

  --interrupt alone only reaches the current default shell/interactive
  request, not independent exec/login/SFTP jobs or forwards. Idle is a no-op.
  --job ID selects one task from --jobs with --interrupt or --stop.
  --stop without --job closes the default channel and every job channel.

  A session dies with its connection: a jump host or the target rebooting
  ends it, and the next call says so.  --reconnect N, on the call that
  starts one, builds the chain again instead -- up to N times, 2s, 4s, 8s,
  16s then 30s apart (a gap between attempts, not a period: one that has
  to time out costs up to -o SEC per hop on top, so the window is longer
  than the gaps and never shorter):
  mssh --session s --reconnect 20 -j ops@10.0.0.1 root@10.0.0.9
  The connection comes back; the shell does not.  A new shell starts in the
  home directory with none of the old environment or background jobs, so
  the first command after a reconnect is told so on stderr before it runs
  -- a 'cd /var/log' from before the reboot is gone, and a script that
  assumed otherwise would grep the wrong directory and call it a success.
  It needs an authentication that works with nobody watching (a key, or a
  password written into the endpoint): the daemon is detached, so a
  password that was typed at the prompt cannot be typed again.  A -c
  session is refused too -- its state is the program, and a reconnect
  would start a new one that has none of it.  A session that will not
  reconnect does not vanish: it stays until a command collects the reason,
  and ends then.

  Output streams as the command produces it, so a long-running command
  (a poller, a tail, a build) prints as it goes.  --wait bounds how long
  to keep watching, 0 for no bound; a timeout leaves the session usable
  and the next command stops whatever was still running.  Ctrl-C
  interrupts the remote command rather than killing the local one, so the
  session stays usable; press it twice to walk away and leave it running.

  The session runs behind a pty, so output is stdout and stderr merged in
  the order the program wrote them, and the exit code -- not the stream --
  is what says whether a command failed.  With nothing piped in, a command's
  stdin is /dev/null, so a bare 'cat' ends instead of hanging the session.  A
  program that paginates will stall at its pager until --interrupt, so
  turn paging off in the program itself (gdb: -ex 'set pagination off').

  --exec runs a command on a channel of its own instead of in the shell,
  for the many commands that want the chain but no context:
  mssh --session s --exec 'grep -c error /var/log/messages'
  mssh --session s --exec 'cat big.log' 2>/dev/null   # stderr is separate
  It shares the connection, not the shell, so a 'cd' from an earlier call
  does NOT apply and neither do its variables or functions.  In exchange
  there is no pty: stdout and stderr stay apart, nothing is framed and no
  carriage returns are added, and several --exec commands can run at once,
  even while the session's own shell is busy.  --wait, Ctrl-C, stdin and the
  exit code behave as they do without it; --interrupt requires --job ID.
  A command cut short -- a timeout, an interrupt, a client that walked away --
  is killed with its channel rather than left running on the target.

  --exec -t asks for a pty on that channel, for the programs that refuse
  to run without one, and then it is a login rather than a command:
  mssh --session s --exec -t 'vim /etc/hosts'
  mssh --session s --exec -t top
  Your terminal becomes the program's input, goes raw until it ends,
  resizing your window resizes the remote one, and --wait
  defaults to 0, because time spent in an editor is not evidence that
  anything is stuck.  Both your stdin and your stdout have to be a terminal
  for any of that; redirect either and the command runs without a pty, the
  way it does without -t.

  Sessions live in ~/.mssh/sessions, one socket each, mode 0600: holding
  one is equivalent to being logged in on the target.

  The first call keeps your terminal until the chain is up, so a hop that
  needs a password still asks for one, and -vv shows each hop; only
  then does it detach.

first-time key setup (then no more passwords):
  ssh-keygen -t ed25519                       # if you have no key yet
  mssh --copy-id -j ops:pw1@10.0.0.1 root:pw2@10.0.0.9
  mssh -j ops@10.0.0.1 root@10.0.0.9          # subsequent logins, key auth

key types:
  ed25519 (recommended), ecdsa (nistp256/384/521) and rsa.  DSA is gone:
  paramiko 5 dropped it, as did OpenSSH 9.8.  An OpenSSH certificate is
  used automatically when <key>-cert.pub sits next to the private key.

idle connections:
  mssh sends a keepalive every 30s (-k SEC, 0 to disable), so an idle
  session survives NAT and firewall timeouts.  It cannot override a
  server-side ClientAliveInterval or a shell TMOUT -- those still apply.

security note:
  passwords on the command line are visible in `ps` and shell history.
  Prefer keys, or omit the password and let mssh prompt for it.

legacy servers:
  against OpenSSH older than 7.8, RSA keys cannot be used: those servers
  accept only SHA-1 'ssh-rsa' signatures, which paramiko 5 no longer
  produces. Use an ed25519 key (ssh-keygen -t ed25519) instead.
"""


def build_parser():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="mssh",
        description="Multi-hop SSH login through one or more jump hosts.",
        epilog=EPILOG,
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-j", "--jump", action="append", default=[], metavar="SPEC",
        help="jump host as [user[:password]@]host[:port]; repeat for each hop, in order",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "args", metavar="TARGET|PATH", nargs="*",
        help="either one login target, [user[:password]@]host[:port] with the "
             "user defaulting to the local one, or copy arguments SOURCE... "
             "DEST in which the remote side carries that same endpoint with "
             "':' and a path appended: "
             "'./app.tar root@10.0.0.9:/tmp/' uploads, "
             "'root@10.0.0.9:/var/log/syslog ./' downloads.  The name of a "
             "live session stands in for a target here, and then anything "
             "after it is a command for that session",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-r", "--recursive", action="store_true",
        help="copy directories recursively",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-p", "--preserve", action="store_true",
        help="preserve the exact mode and the modification time on copied "
             "files.  Without it a copy still keeps its permissions, as scp "
             "does, but your umask filters them",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-c", "--command", metavar="CMD",
        help="run CMD on the target and exit instead of opening a shell",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--rcfile", metavar="FILE",
        help="upload a local shell script to a private target /tmp file and "
             "source it silently; sessions retain it for logins, reupload on "
             "reconnect and remove it on stop; direct logins remove it after source",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-i", "--identity", action="append", metavar="KEYFILE",
        help="private key file to authenticate with, e.g. ~/.ssh/id_rsa; "
             "repeat to offer several. Applies to every hop. Prompts once "
             "for the passphrase if the key is encrypted",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--copy-id", nargs="?", const="", metavar="PUBKEY",
        help="install a public key into ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on every hop, "
             "then exit without opening a shell or running -c, so later "
             "connections need no password. "
             "Defaults to ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub or id_rsa.pub",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-t", "--force-tty", action="store_true",
        help="allocate a pty even with -c.  With --session it applies to "
             "--exec only, for a program that refuses to run without a "
             "terminal (vim, top, an interactive installer): the pty is "
             "requested, your terminal is forwarded as its input, goes "
             "raw for the length of the command, resizing your window "
             "resizes the remote one, and --wait defaults to 0 since you are "
             "sitting at the program.  A session's own shell already has a "
             "pty, so the flag has no meaning there, and a login ('mssh NAME' "
             "with nothing to run) already does all of it",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-n", "--no-stdin", action="store_true",
        help="never forward stdin; the remote command reads /dev/null.  With "
             "--session, stdin is otherwise forwarded whenever it is a pipe or "
             "a file, so use this when this process's stdin belongs to "
             "something else, such as a script being read from a pipe",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-o", "--timeout", type=float, default=DEFAULT_TIMEOUT, metavar="SEC",
        help="per-hop connect timeout in seconds (default: %d)" % DEFAULT_TIMEOUT,
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-k", "--keepalive", type=int, default=30, metavar="SEC",
        help="keepalive interval, 0 to disable (default: 30)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-C", "--compress", action="store_true", help="enable compression",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--strict", action="store_true",
        help="reject unknown host keys instead of auto-adding them",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--known-hosts", metavar="FILE", help="known_hosts file to load",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--no-agent", action="store_true", help="do not use ssh-agent",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--no-keys", action="store_true", help="do not look for keys in ~/.ssh",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--no-prompt", action="store_true",
        help="never prompt for a missing password (fail instead)",
    )
    group = parser.add_argument_group("persistent sessions")
    group.add_argument(
        "--session", metavar="NAME",
        help="keep the connection alive under NAME.  The first call carries "
             "the chain and detaches a local daemon holding a remote shell; "
             "every later 'mssh NAME CMD' runs CMD in that same shell, so the "
             "working directory, variables and any loaded state persist "
             "between calls.  Once the session is live this flag may be left "
             "out -- the name is the first word, as a host would be, since it "
             "names the same machine -- and 'mssh NAME' with nothing to run "
             "is a login on a channel of its own",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--sessions", "--list", action="store_true",
        help="list live sessions with their job counts and exit",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--stop", action="store_true",
        help="end the named session and close its connection; with --job ID, "
             "cancel only that task and keep the session alive",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--interrupt", action="store_true",
        help="interrupt the default shell's task; use --job ID for a specific task",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--job", type=int, metavar="ID",
        help="with --interrupt or --stop, target a job ID from --jobs",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--jobs", action="store_true",
        help="list active session commands, exec/login channels, transfers "
             "and local forwards; "
             "exclude the default persistent shell",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--status", action="store_true",
        help="print session state, link, uptime, activity and task counts, "
             "including default job state and reconnect progress",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "-L", dest="local_forwards", action="append", default=[],
        metavar="SPEC",
        help="add a persistent local TCP/Unix socket forward (SSH -L syntax) "
             "to a session; repeat for "
             "several, default bind is localhost; --jobs lists forwards and "
             "--interrupt --job ID stops one",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "-D", dest="dynamic_forwards", action="append", default=[],
        metavar="[BIND:]PORT",
        help="add a persistent SOCKS4/5 TCP proxy to a session; repeat for "
             "several, default bind is localhost; remote DNS, no authentication; "
             "--jobs lists proxies and --stop --job ID stops one",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--prompt", metavar="REGEX",
        help="with --session -c: the regex marking the end of the program's "
             r"reply, e.g. '\(gdb\) $' or '>>> $'.  Without it mssh guesses, "
             "treating a --idle pause in the output as the end",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--stdin", action="store_true",
        help="also forward stdin when it is a terminal, so the remote command "
             "reads what you type until you end it with Ctrl-D.  A pipe or a "
             "file is forwarded without this; a terminal is not, because the "
             "Ctrl-D would then be needed by every command, 'ls' included",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--exec", dest="exec_channel", action="store_true",
        help="run the command on a channel of its own instead of in the "
             "session's shell.  It shares the connection but not the shell, "
             "so the session's working directory, variables and functions do "
             "NOT apply -- in exchange stdout and stderr come back separately "
             "(no pty merges them), nothing is framed, and several such "
             "commands can run at once.  Use it for the many commands that "
             "need the chain but no context",
    )
    copies = group.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    copies.add_argument(
        "--put", nargs=2, metavar=("LOCAL", "REMOTE"),
        help="upload a local file or directory through an existing session; "
             "supports -r and -p",
    )
    copies.add_argument(
        "--get", nargs=2, metavar=("REMOTE", "LOCAL"),
        help="download a remote file or directory through an existing session; "
             "supports -r and -p",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--idle", type=float, default=DEFAULT_SESSION_IDLE, metavar="SEC",
        help="without --prompt, how long output must pause before it counts "
             "as finished (default: %g)" % DEFAULT_SESSION_IDLE,
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--wait", type=float, default=None, metavar="SEC",
        help="how long to wait for a command to finish; its output streams "
             "as it arrives either way, and the session survives a timeout.  "
             "0 waits indefinitely, so a command that never ends keeps "
             "printing until you Ctrl-C it (default: %g, or 0 for a login and "
             "for --exec -t, where you are sitting at the program)"
             % DEFAULT_SESSION_WAIT,
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--reconnect", type=int, default=0, metavar="N",
        help="on the call that starts the session: if the connection drops, "
             "rebuild the whole chain, up to N times, leaving 2s, 4s, 8s, "
             "16s then 30s BETWEEN attempts.  That is a gap, not a period: "
             "an attempt that has to time out adds its own cost on top (up "
             "to -o SEC per hop, three times over in the worst case), so N "
             "attempts span at least 2+4+8+16+30*(N-4) seconds and usually "
             "more -- --reconnect 20 is eight minutes of waiting plus "
             "however long twenty failures take, which is the order of a "
             "physical machine's reboot.  The remote SHELL cannot be "
             "rebuilt, only the connection, so the first command after a "
             "reconnect is told on stderr that the working directory, the "
             "environment and any background jobs are gone.  Needs an "
             "authentication that works with nobody watching -- a key, or a "
             "password in the endpoint -- and is refused for a -c session, "
             "whose state is the program itself (default: 0, off)",
    )
    group.add_argument(
        "--session-idle", type=float, default=0, metavar="SEC",
        help="end the session after SEC of no commands, so a forgotten one "
             "does not hold a connection open forever (default: 0, never)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-v", "--verbose", action="count", default=0,
        help="stderr logging: -v WARNING, -vv INFO, -vvv DEBUG",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-V", "--version", action="version", version="mssh " + __version__,
    )
    return parser


def parse_cli(parser, argv):
    """parse_args, except that words may come on either side of an option.

    argparse takes the one positional list from the first run of plain words
    and calls everything after the next option "unrecognized", so 'mssh host
    --wait 5 cmd' and 'mssh host --exec -t -- ls -l' were refused although
    each part is fine.  The words it set aside are the continuation of that
    list, so they are put back after it; a '--' ends the options, as it does
    everywhere else, and what follows is the command whatever it looks like.
    """
    opts, extras = parser.parse_known_args(argv)
    words, literal = [], False
    for index, item in enumerate(extras):
        if literal or item == "-" or not item.startswith("-"):
            words.append(item)
        elif item == "--":
            literal = True
        else:
            hint = ""
            if opts.args or words:
                hint = (" (to pass %s to the remote command, put -- before "
                        "it: mssh NAME -- cmd %s)" % (item, item))
            parser.error("unrecognized arguments: %s%s"
                         % (" ".join(extras[index:]), hint))
    opts.args = list(opts.args) + words
    return opts


def _takes_value(parser, token):
    """Whether the option spelled `token` consumes the word after it.

    Only to find where the words start, so anything it cannot place counts as
    a flag; argparse still has the final say on what the options mean.
    """
    acts = parser._option_string_actions
    if token.startswith("--"):
        if "=" in token:
            return False
        action = acts.get(token)
        if action is None:
            hits = [a for o, a in acts.items() if o.startswith(token)]
            action = hits[0] if len({id(a) for a in hits}) == 1 else None
        return action is not None and action.nargs != 0
    for pos in range(1, len(token)):
        action = acts.get("-" + token[pos])
        if action is None:
            return False
        if action.nargs != 0:
            return pos == len(token) - 1
    return False


def _split_command(parser, argv):
    """Cut 'mssh NAME [options] cmd ...' where the command begins.

    Once the first word names a live session, the next word that is not an
    option is the command and so is everything after it, whatever it looks
    like: 'mssh vm1 ls -l' runs 'ls -l', as ssh would.  Options go before the
    command ('mssh vm1 --wait 5 sleep 3'); after it they are the command's.
    Returns (what mssh parses, the command's words).  A call that does not
    name a session this way is left whole.
    """
    flags = ("--session", "--sessions", "--copy-id")
    first = None
    index = 0
    while index < len(argv):
        token = argv[index]
        if token == "--":
            if first is not None:
                return argv[:index + 1], argv[index + 1:]
            return argv, []
        if token.startswith("-") and token != "-":
            if token.split("=")[0] in flags:
                return argv, []
            if token in ("--put", "--get"):
                index += 3
                continue
            index += 2 if _takes_value(parser, token) else 1
            continue
        if first is None:
            first = token
            index += 1
            continue
        if _live_session_named(first):
            return argv[:index], argv[index:]
        return argv, []
    return argv, []


def main(argv=None):
    parser = build_parser()
    if argv is None:
        argv = sys.argv[1:]
    head, command = _split_command(parser, list(argv))
    opts = parse_cli(parser, head)
    opts.args = list(opts.args) + command

    if opts.job is not None and (
            not (opts.interrupt or opts.stop) or opts.job < 0):
        parser.error("--job requires --interrupt or --stop and a nonnegative job ID")

    try:
        opts.local_forwards = [
            _local_forward_spec(parse_local_forward(spec))
            for spec in opts.local_forwards]
        for spec in opts.dynamic_forwards:
            parse_dynamic_forward(spec)
    except ValueError as exc:
        parser.error(str(exc))

    opts.rcfile_data = None
    if opts.rcfile:
        if opts.command or opts.copy_id is not None or opts.sessions \
                or opts.stop or opts.interrupt or opts.status or opts.jobs \
                or opts.exec_channel:
            parser.error("--rcfile only applies to logins and shell session creation")
        try:
            with open(os.path.expanduser(opts.rcfile), "rb") as source:
                opts.rcfile_data = source.read()
        except OSError as exc:
            parser.error("cannot read --rcfile %r: %s" % (opts.rcfile, exc))

    # --session is optional once the session exists: a session's name is also
    # the name of the machine it reached, so 'mssh host', 'mssh host cmd' and
    # 'mssh host --stop' mean the same as spelling --session out.  Being live
    # is the whole test and nothing is guessed from the shape of the word: a
    # name no session answers to is read as a host, exactly as before, which is
    # what keeps 'mssh 10.0.0.9' and every existing command line working.
    # --copy-id is left out of it -- it builds a chain of its own, so its
    # argument is a host by definition.
    if not opts.session and not opts.sessions and opts.args \
            and opts.copy_id is None and _live_session_named(opts.args[0]):
        opts.session = opts.args.pop(0)

    if opts.wait is None:
        # A login is not a command: somebody is sitting at the program, and the
        # time they spend in a shell, an editor or a debugger is not evidence
        # that anything is stuck.  A deadline that cut it off mid-use would be
        # the opposite of what asking for a terminal meant, so 'mssh host' and
        # --exec -t wait as long as the program lives, and every other case
        # keeps the deadline that catches a command which never ends.
        opts.wait = 0.0 if (_session_login_wanted(opts)
                            or (opts.exec_channel and opts.force_tty)) \
            else DEFAULT_SESSION_WAIT

    if opts.sessions:
        if opts.local_forwards or opts.dynamic_forwards:
            parser.error("-L/-D cannot be combined with --sessions")
        if opts.job is not None:
            parser.error("--job cannot be combined with --sessions")
        if opts.jobs:
            parser.error("--jobs cannot be combined with --sessions")
        if opts.put is not None or opts.get is not None:
            parser.error("--put/--get require an existing session")
        if opts.session or opts.args:
            parser.error("--sessions lists sessions and exits; it takes no "
                         "other arguments")
            return 2
        return run_session_list()

    if opts.session:
        return run_session(parser, opts)

    if opts.local_forwards:
        parser.error("-L requires a session: "
                     "mssh --session NAME -L PORT:HOST:HOSTPORT TARGET")
    if opts.dynamic_forwards:
        parser.error("-D requires a session: "
                     "mssh --session NAME -D [BIND:]PORT TARGET")
    if opts.put is not None or opts.get is not None:
        parser.error("--put/--get require an existing session: "
                     "mssh NAME --put LOCAL REMOTE")

    for flag in ("stop", "interrupt", "status", "jobs"):
        if getattr(opts, flag):
            # Either no name was given or the one given is not live; both end
            # here, so the message names the live ones rather than guessing
            # which of the two happened.
            parser.error("--%s acts on a live session: 'mssh NAME --%s' "
                         "(--sessions lists them)" % (flag, flag))
            return 2
    if opts.prompt is not None:
        parser.error("--prompt only applies to --session -c")
        return 2
    if opts.reconnect:
        parser.error("--reconnect keeps a session's chain alive across a "
                     "reboot, so it belongs on the call that starts one: "
                     "mssh --session NAME --reconnect %d [-j <jump>...] "
                     "<target>" % opts.reconnect)
        return 2
    if opts.exec_channel:
        parser.error("--exec runs a command in a session's connection; name "
                     "the session with --session NAME (without a session, a "
                     "plain -c already runs on its own channel)")
        return 2

    if not opts.args:
        parser.error("no target given; pass [user[:password]@]host[:port] "
                     "(mssh --help for the full syntax)")
        return 2

    # One positional is a login target; several mean a copy, and then the
    # target is whichever host the remote paths name.
    opts.paths = opts.args if len(opts.args) > 1 else []
    if opts.rcfile and opts.paths:
        parser.error("--rcfile cannot be combined with a file copy")
    plan = None

    if not opts.paths:
        # A lone argument that carries a path is a half-written copy.  Say so,
        # rather than letting parse_endpoint complain about the path looking
        # like a port.  --copy-id is the usual culprit: its optional argument
        # silently eats the source.
        try:
            lone_ep, _ = split_copy_arg(opts.args[0])
        except ValueError:
            lone_ep = None
        if lone_ep is not None:
            extra = ""
            if opts.copy_id:
                extra = (" (--copy-id took %r as its public key; put it "
                         "after the paths, or use --copy-id=FILE)"
                         % opts.copy_id)
            parser.error("%s names a remote file, so this is a copy, but "
                         "only one path was given -- a copy needs SOURCE "
                         "and DEST%s" % (opts.args[0], extra))
            return 2

    try:
        jumps = [parse_endpoint(spec) for spec in opts.jump]
        if opts.paths:
            plan = plan_copy(opts.paths)
            target = plan[3]
        else:
            target = parse_endpoint(opts.args[0])
    except ValueError as exc:
        parser.error(str(exc))
        return 2

    # --copy-id with no argument: fall back to a standard public key.
    if opts.copy_id == "":
        found = _default_pubkey()
        if found is None:
            parser.error(
                "no public key found in ~/.ssh. Generate one with:\n"
                "    ssh-keygen -t ed25519\n"
                "  (press Enter at each prompt for a passwordless key), "
                "or pass --copy-id <file>"
            )
            return 2
        opts.copy_id = found

    # Reject the impossible combinations before opening any connection.
    if opts.paths:
        if opts.command:
            parser.error("-c and a file copy are mutually exclusive")
            return 2
        if opts.copy_id:
            parser.error("--copy-id installs a key and exits; it cannot also "
                         "copy files")
            return 2
    elif opts.recursive or opts.preserve:
        parser.error("-r/-p only apply to a file copy; pass SOURCE and DEST")
        return 2

    _setup_logging(opts)

    clients = []
    try:
        clients, tgt = build_chain(jumps, target, opts)
        # --copy-id is a setup action, not a login: installing the key on
        # every hop is the whole job, so stop before any shell or command.
        if opts.copy_id:
            return 0
        if opts.paths:
            return run_copy(tgt, plan, opts)
        if opts.command:
            return run_command(tgt, opts.command, opts)
        return run_interactive(tgt, opts)
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.stderr.write("\nmssh: interrupted\n")
        return 130
    finally:
        close_all(clients)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
